1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,967
In 10 days in 1854, more than 500 people died of

2
00:00:04,967 --> 00:00:07,860
cholera in a few streets of London.

3
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:12,008
Almost all of them lived within about 250 yards of

4
00:00:12,008 --> 00:00:13,784
one thing. A water pump.

5
00:00:14,234 --> 00:00:16,256
Most doctors of the day blamed bad air.

6
00:00:16,256 --> 00:00:17,709
One doctor blamed the water.

7
00:00:17,709 --> 00:00:19,731
And he went house by house to prove it.

8
00:00:20,181 --> 00:00:23,240
The famous version of this story says a map stopped

9
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:24,020
the outbreak.

10
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:25,520
The real story is better.

11
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,939
This is how John Snow traced a deadly cholera

12
00:00:28,939 --> 00:00:30,684
outbreak to a single pump.

13
00:00:33,084 --> 00:00:34,325
Start with the disease.

14
00:00:35,325 --> 00:00:38,359
Cholera is caused by a bacterium, Vibrio cholerae.

15
00:00:38,359 --> 00:00:41,089
The World Health Organization says it spreads

16
00:00:41,089 --> 00:00:43,698
through food or water contaminated with it.

17
00:00:44,148 --> 00:00:46,810
For most of history, nobody knew that.

18
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:49,752
A cholera epidemic began in India in 1817.

19
00:00:49,752 --> 00:00:53,324
It reached Britain in 1831, and that first epidemic

20
00:00:53,324 --> 00:00:55,286
killed around 30,000 people.

21
00:00:55,736 --> 00:00:58,202
In 1832, it reached New York.

22
00:00:58,202 --> 00:01:01,178
A city of more than 200,000 people.

23
00:01:01,178 --> 00:01:04,239
About half of them fled. 3,513 died.

24
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:05,666
What caused it?

25
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:07,620
The leading theory was miasma.

26
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:11,268
Disease, doctors believed, came from breathing foul air:

27
00:01:11,268 --> 00:01:13,026
the stink of filth and rot.

28
00:01:13,476 --> 00:01:16,765
A London doctor named John Snow did not believe it.

29
00:01:16,765 --> 00:01:18,893
Snow was a pioneer of anesthesia.

30
00:01:18,893 --> 00:01:22,117
In the 1850s, he gave chloroform to Queen Victoria

31
00:01:22,117 --> 00:01:24,761
during the births of two of her children.

32
00:01:25,211 --> 00:01:28,378
In 1849, during another London epidemic, Snow

33
00:01:28,378 --> 00:01:32,247
published his theory that cholera spread through water.

34
00:01:32,247 --> 00:01:33,795
It was mostly ignored.

35
00:01:34,295 --> 00:01:36,894
Five years later, he got a test he never wanted.

36
00:01:37,894 --> 00:01:41,835
On the night of August 31, 1854, cholera exploded in

37
00:01:41,835 --> 00:01:45,396
Soho, a crowded neighborhood in central London.

38
00:01:45,846 --> 00:01:48,832
Snow later called it, probably, the most terrible

39
00:01:48,832 --> 00:01:51,818
outbreak of cholera ever to occur in the kingdom.

40
00:01:52,268 --> 00:01:55,225
On September 1 alone, 143 people were struck.

41
00:01:55,225 --> 00:01:58,642
Within six days, the worst-hit streets had lost more

42
00:01:58,642 --> 00:02:01,206
than three-quarters of their residents.

43
00:02:01,656 --> 00:02:04,781
Snow suspected the water right away, and the pump on

44
00:02:04,781 --> 00:02:05,563
Broad Street.

45
00:02:05,563 --> 00:02:09,050
When he first looked at its water, it seemed almost clear.

46
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:12,476
Over the next 2 days, he saw small white particles in it.

47
00:02:12,926 --> 00:02:15,818
Clear-looking water can still carry germs.

48
00:02:15,818 --> 00:02:19,329
A 1943 Army training film made the same point under

49
00:02:19,329 --> 00:02:20,224
a microscope:

50
00:02:20,224 --> 00:02:23,805
water that looks clean can be, quote, alive with all

51
00:02:23,805 --> 00:02:25,388
sorts of living matter.

52
00:02:25,838 --> 00:02:28,510
So Snow got the list of cholera deaths from the

53
00:02:28,510 --> 00:02:29,874
General Register Office.

54
00:02:29,874 --> 00:02:32,262
83 deaths in the last 3 days of that week.

55
00:02:32,262 --> 00:02:33,854
Then he went house by house.

56
00:02:34,304 --> 00:02:37,239
Nearly all the deaths had happened a short distance

57
00:02:37,239 --> 00:02:38,044
from the pump.

58
00:02:38,044 --> 00:02:40,980
Only 10 were in houses clearly nearer another pump.

59
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:43,742
In 5 of those, the families told him they always

60
00:02:43,742 --> 00:02:45,641
sent for water from Broad Street.

61
00:02:45,641 --> 00:02:46,677
They preferred it.

62
00:02:46,677 --> 00:02:49,785
3 more were children who went to school near the pump.

63
00:02:50,235 --> 00:02:52,592
Then he looked at the people who did not get sick.

64
00:02:53,042 --> 00:02:56,208
The workhouse on Poland Street held 535 people.

65
00:02:56,208 --> 00:02:59,712
It was almost surrounded by houses where people died

66
00:02:59,712 --> 00:03:00,453
of cholera.

67
00:03:00,453 --> 00:03:02,272
Only 5 of its inmates died.

68
00:03:02,272 --> 00:03:04,360
The workhouse had its own well.

69
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:07,600
A brewery on Broad Street, near the pump, had more

70
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,493
than 70 workmen.

71
00:03:08,493 --> 00:03:11,394
None of them had cholera, at least in a severe form.

72
00:03:11,394 --> 00:03:14,574
The owner told Snow his men drank malt liquor, not water.

73
00:03:14,574 --> 00:03:16,973
And the brewery had a deep well of its own.

74
00:03:17,423 --> 00:03:19,239
And a case far away fit too.

75
00:03:19,239 --> 00:03:22,417
In Hampstead, a widow who had not been near Broad

76
00:03:22,417 --> 00:03:25,595
Street for months had a large bottle of its water

77
00:03:25,595 --> 00:03:28,903
brought to her every day, because she preferred it.

78
00:03:28,903 --> 00:03:30,200
She died of cholera.

79
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:33,508
So did a niece who visited her and drank the water.

80
00:03:33,508 --> 00:03:36,881
At the time, there was no cholera in either of their

81
00:03:36,881 --> 00:03:37,789
neighborhoods.

82
00:03:38,239 --> 00:03:41,043
On the evening of Thursday, September 7, Snow

83
00:03:41,043 --> 00:03:44,532
presented his evidence to the parish Board of Guardians.

84
00:03:44,982 --> 00:03:46,630
Snow wrote what happened next.

85
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:49,332
That's where the famous version ends.

86
00:03:49,332 --> 00:03:51,887
Snow drew a map, the handle came off, and the

87
00:03:51,887 --> 00:03:52,852
outbreak stopped.

88
00:03:52,852 --> 00:03:55,465
Almost every part of that is more complicated.

89
00:03:56,465 --> 00:03:58,229
First, the map came later.

90
00:03:58,229 --> 00:04:01,961
Snow first showed a map of the deaths in December 1854.

91
00:04:01,961 --> 00:04:04,133
It appeared in his book in 1855.

92
00:04:04,133 --> 00:04:06,643
His suspicion of the pump came first.

93
00:04:07,093 --> 00:04:10,248
On the map, each black bar is a death at that address.

94
00:04:10,248 --> 00:04:13,577
You can see them stacked around the pump on Broad Street.

95
00:04:14,027 --> 00:04:16,868
Second, the outbreak was already fading.

96
00:04:16,868 --> 00:04:18,360
Snow said so himself.

97
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:21,911
New cases had fallen from 143 on September 1 to 12

98
00:04:21,911 --> 00:04:24,965
on the 8th, the day the handle was removed.

99
00:04:25,415 --> 00:04:26,691
Many people had fled.

100
00:04:26,691 --> 00:04:29,790
Snow wrote that cases had dropped so far before the

101
00:04:29,790 --> 00:04:32,828
water was stopped that it was impossible to decide

102
00:04:32,828 --> 00:04:35,441
whether the well still carried the disease.

103
00:04:35,891 --> 00:04:37,863
And most experts were not convinced.

104
00:04:37,863 --> 00:04:40,987
A government Board of Health report dismissed his theory.

105
00:04:41,437 --> 00:04:44,416
The strongest support came from a man who expected

106
00:04:44,416 --> 00:04:45,608
to prove Snow wrong.

107
00:04:45,608 --> 00:04:48,468
The Reverend Henry Whitehead, a local clergyman.

108
00:04:48,918 --> 00:04:52,557
In the spring of 1855, Whitehead found what was

109
00:04:52,557 --> 00:04:54,260
likely the first case:

110
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:58,208
a 5-month-old baby at 40 Broad Street, right beside

111
00:04:58,208 --> 00:05:00,840
the pump, who died on September 2.

112
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:03,976
The baby's mother had soaked the soiled diapers in

113
00:05:03,976 --> 00:05:06,662
water and emptied it into the cesspool in front of

114
00:05:06,662 --> 00:05:07,199
the house.

115
00:05:07,649 --> 00:05:10,340
When the surveyor dug it out, the cesspool was 2

116
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:11,909
feet 8 inches from the well.

117
00:05:11,909 --> 00:05:14,432
Its decayed brickwork let waste seep into the

118
00:05:14,432 --> 00:05:15,273
drinking water.

119
00:05:15,723 --> 00:05:18,734
Whitehead admitted he had come, slowly and, as he

120
00:05:18,734 --> 00:05:21,807
put it, reluctantly, to link the pump water to the

121
00:05:21,807 --> 00:05:22,360
outbreak.

122
00:05:22,810 --> 00:05:26,046
In August 1855, the parish committee concluded the

123
00:05:26,046 --> 00:05:29,216
outbreak was, in some manner, attributable to the

124
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:32,581
use of the impure water of the well on Broad Street.

125
00:05:32,581 --> 00:05:34,264
The vote was split evenly.

126
00:05:34,264 --> 00:05:36,593
The chairman cast the deciding vote.

127
00:05:37,093 --> 00:05:39,464
Snow had a second piece of evidence.

128
00:05:39,464 --> 00:05:41,440
And it covered 300,000 people.

129
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:45,693
In south London, two water companies ran their pipes

130
00:05:45,693 --> 00:05:47,069
down the same streets.

131
00:05:47,069 --> 00:05:50,322
In 1852, the Lambeth Company moved its intake up the

132
00:05:50,322 --> 00:05:53,262
Thames, to Thames Ditton, getting water free of

133
00:05:53,262 --> 00:05:54,263
London's sewage.

134
00:05:54,263 --> 00:05:57,390
The Southwark and Vauxhall Company still drew from

135
00:05:57,390 --> 00:05:58,892
the Thames at Battersea.

136
00:05:59,342 --> 00:06:02,356
Each company supplied rich and poor, large houses

137
00:06:02,356 --> 00:06:02,972
and small.

138
00:06:02,972 --> 00:06:06,171
Snow saw no difference between the two groups except

139
00:06:06,171 --> 00:06:06,786
the water.

140
00:06:07,236 --> 00:06:09,775
He called it an experiment on the grandest scale.

141
00:06:10,225 --> 00:06:11,439
Then he counted.

142
00:06:11,439 --> 00:06:15,156
In the first 7 weeks of the 1854 epidemic, houses

143
00:06:15,156 --> 00:06:18,949
supplied by Southwark and Vauxhall had 315 cholera

144
00:06:18,949 --> 00:06:20,845
deaths per 10,000 houses.

145
00:06:20,845 --> 00:06:23,425
Houses supplied by Lambeth had 37.

146
00:06:23,875 --> 00:06:27,349
Snow concluded that the death rate was between 8 and

147
00:06:27,349 --> 00:06:29,889
9 times higher with the dirtier water.

148
00:06:30,389 --> 00:06:32,354
But Snow never saw the germ itself.

149
00:06:33,354 --> 00:06:35,161
Someone else already had.

150
00:06:35,161 --> 00:06:38,703
In 1854, the Italian anatomist Filippo Pacini saw

151
00:06:38,703 --> 00:06:42,606
and described the cholera pathogen under a microscope.

152
00:06:43,056 --> 00:06:46,193
Then, at the end of 1883, the German scientist

153
00:06:46,193 --> 00:06:49,603
Robert Koch traveled to Calcutta, India, during an

154
00:06:49,603 --> 00:06:50,217
outbreak.

155
00:06:50,217 --> 00:06:53,900
At the beginning of 1884, he identified the bacterium.

156
00:06:54,350 --> 00:06:57,345
John Snow did not live to see it.

157
00:06:57,345 --> 00:06:59,886
He died in June 1858, at 45.

158
00:07:00,336 --> 00:07:03,403
That summer, London had what became known as the

159
00:07:03,403 --> 00:07:04,170
Great Stink:

160
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:07,045
hot weather and sewage exposed in the Thames.

161
00:07:07,045 --> 00:07:10,240
It pushed the city to build a modern sewer system,

162
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:12,987
overseen by the engineer Joseph Bazalgette.

163
00:07:13,437 --> 00:07:16,387
London's final cholera outbreak came in 1866.

164
00:07:16,387 --> 00:07:19,796
It hit parts of east London that drew water from the

165
00:07:19,796 --> 00:07:23,008
Old Ford Reservoir, and were not connected to the

166
00:07:23,008 --> 00:07:23,729
new sewers.

167
00:07:24,229 --> 00:07:26,386
In America, the lesson became chlorine.

168
00:07:27,386 --> 00:07:30,869
In 1908, Jersey City, New Jersey, became the first U.S.

169
00:07:30,869 --> 00:07:34,478
city to begin routine disinfection of its drinking water.

170
00:07:34,478 --> 00:07:37,771
Thousands of cities and towns followed over the next

171
00:07:37,771 --> 00:07:38,215
decade.

172
00:07:38,665 --> 00:07:42,820
The CDC says diseases like cholera and typhoid

173
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:44,717
dropped dramatically.

174
00:07:44,717 --> 00:07:49,324
Typhoid fever went from about 100 cases per 100,000

175
00:07:49,324 --> 00:07:52,124
people in 1900, to 0.1 in 2006.

176
00:07:52,574 --> 00:07:55,710
Cities cleaned their water by aerating, settling,

177
00:07:55,710 --> 00:07:57,631
filtering and chlorinating it.

178
00:07:57,631 --> 00:08:01,023
That's how the Army explained it to soldiers in 1943.

179
00:08:01,473 --> 00:08:04,794
Because in the field, there was no city water plant.

180
00:08:04,794 --> 00:08:07,604
So soldiers hung a canvas bag from a tripod.

181
00:08:07,604 --> 00:08:08,817
It held 36 gallons.

182
00:08:09,267 --> 00:08:12,024
They treated the water with chlorine, then tested a

183
00:08:12,024 --> 00:08:13,807
sample to check there was enough.

184
00:08:13,807 --> 00:08:16,510
The film says it took 30 minutes for the chemicals

185
00:08:16,510 --> 00:08:17,267
to kill germs.

186
00:08:17,717 --> 00:08:20,839
Today, Army units still train to make their own

187
00:08:20,839 --> 00:08:21,836
drinking water.

188
00:08:21,836 --> 00:08:24,893
In August 2026, during field training in South

189
00:08:24,893 --> 00:08:28,215
Korea, soldiers pumped water from the Imjin River,

190
00:08:28,215 --> 00:08:31,338
tested it and purified it with a Tactical Water

191
00:08:31,338 --> 00:08:32,667
Purification System.

192
00:08:33,117 --> 00:08:36,610
Preventive medicine soldiers test the water troops drink.

193
00:08:36,610 --> 00:08:39,735
In Kosovo, they tested water at liaison team houses

194
00:08:39,735 --> 00:08:41,818
to make sure it was safe to drink.

195
00:08:42,268 --> 00:08:44,941
And sometimes the water goes to civilians.

196
00:08:44,941 --> 00:08:48,060
During a drought in Puerto Rico in 2026, National

197
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:51,434
Guard soldiers purified and delivered drinking water.

198
00:08:51,434 --> 00:08:54,426
More than a million gallons distributed so far.

199
00:08:54,926 --> 00:08:58,797
Today, the CDC says cholera is rare in the United States.

200
00:08:58,797 --> 00:09:02,328
With modern water and sewer treatment systems, there

201
00:09:02,328 --> 00:09:04,501
are typically fewer than 20 U.S.

202
00:09:04,501 --> 00:09:07,081
cases a year, mostly linked to travel.

203
00:09:07,531 --> 00:09:10,032
But the disease is not history.

204
00:09:10,032 --> 00:09:14,065
Researchers cited by the World Health Organization

205
00:09:14,065 --> 00:09:18,017
estimate 1.3 to 4 million cases a year worldwide,

206
00:09:18,017 --> 00:09:20,356
and 21,000 to 143,000 deaths.

207
00:09:20,806 --> 00:09:24,932
In 2025, 47 countries reported cases to the WHO, and

208
00:09:24,932 --> 00:09:28,979
the reported death toll was the highest since 1999.

209
00:09:29,429 --> 00:09:32,736
In 2019, after Cyclone Idai hit Mozambique, the

210
00:09:32,736 --> 00:09:36,255
Defense Department took part in the effort to stop

211
00:09:36,255 --> 00:09:39,773
cholera from spreading, moving relief for the U.S.

212
00:09:39,773 --> 00:09:42,377
Agency for International Development.

213
00:09:42,827 --> 00:09:46,011
The WHO says safe water, basic sanitation and

214
00:09:46,011 --> 00:09:48,911
hygiene are essential to prevent cholera.

215
00:09:48,911 --> 00:09:51,529
John Snow pointed to the water first.

216
00:09:51,979 --> 00:09:55,539
In 1854, a doctor counted deaths, asked where each

217
00:09:55,539 --> 00:09:59,668
family got its water, and followed the evidence to a pump.

218
00:10:00,118 --> 00:10:03,473
Public health teams still count cases and chart them

219
00:10:03,473 --> 00:10:06,440
over time, to see when an outbreak started and

220
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:08,505
whether it is growing or fading.

221
00:10:08,955 --> 00:10:10,166
Next on KEPT ALIVE:

222
00:10:10,166 --> 00:10:13,419
more stories about how medicine keeps people alive.

223
00:10:13,419 --> 00:10:15,523
Subscribe so you don't miss them.

224
00:10:16,323 --> 00:10:17,464
For education only.

225
00:10:17,464 --> 00:10:19,084
This is not medical advice.

226
00:10:19,084 --> 00:10:21,906
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
